                                                         Abstract
            A recurring inference in interpretability research reads: the correct answer out-
            ranks a competitor at some intermediate layer, therefore the model “represents the
            answer internally” and fails only at readout. We show that this inference is not
            merely weak but untestable as stated. The statistic behind it is an existence test
            taken as a maximum over ∼30 noisy comparisons; two exchangeable series cross
            almost surely, and under the natural label-swap null the statistic is invariant, so
            its null distribution is a point mass and no test exists. We demonstrate this first
            in simulation, with no model and no data, and then as a case study on our own
            prior result. That result reported that in 137 of 137 stale-state tracking failures
            in Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, the correct state outranks the stale state in at least one
            internal depth, and concluded the update was encoded but unused. It fails four
            controls. The reverse statistic holds in 137/137; the same test fires in 488/488
            successes; the permutation null is degenerate; and the graded form of the statistic
            is significant in the opposite direction — the correct state wins fewer depths than
            label-swap chance. We further show that two figures we had reported for the
            same quantity, a mean best rank of 47,343 and of 2.3, are the same lens on the
            same 137 examples and differ only in which depths were swept. Separately, the
            failure and success populations are completely confounded on task condition. After
            matching and correcting for depth selection, two signals survive: an attention-mass
            differential at depth 22 (matched AUC 0.973, held-out 0.970) and, provisionally,
            a J-lens margin at depth 5 (matched 0.888, held-out 0.882). Logit-lens margin
            does not survive. Every surviving result is correlational; none establishes that the
            measured quantity participates in producing the output.


1     Introduction
Since the logit lens [nostalgebraist, 2020], a standard move in interpretability has been to decode
a model’s intermediate residual stream into vocabulary space and ask what the model “already
knows” partway through its computation. When the correct answer ranks above a competitor at
some intermediate depth but loses at the output, it is natural to conclude that the information was
present internally and that something went wrong at readout. Claims of this shape appear across the
literature, including in work arguing that language models encode the correct answer while emitting
an incorrect one [Orgad et al., 2024].
The inference has a structural problem that is independent of the model, the lens, and the task. Written
out, the statistic is
                         E = ⊮[ ∃ ℓ ∈ {0, . . . , L − 1} : rℓ (c) < rℓ (s) ] ,
    ∗ Placeholder. No real names are committed to this source file; fill in at camera-ready.




Preprint. Under review at [VENUE — SET BEFORE SUBMISSION].
an existence test over L depths, i.e. a maximum over L noisy comparisons. Three consequences follow.
First, E saturates: two exchangeable rank series cross at least once with probability approaching 1
well before L = 30, so E = 1 carries almost no information. Second, and more seriously, E has no
usable null. Under the natural null — that the labels c and s are exchangeable — if both E(c ≻ s)
and E(s ≻ c) hold in every example, then swapping the labels cannot change the statistic’s value. Its
null distribution is a point mass at the observed value, and there is nothing to test against. Third, E
is blind to magnitude: it is satisfied identically whether the correct answer reaches rank 2 or rank
40,000, and whether the competitor is at rank 1 or rank 150,000.
This paper makes that argument concretely and then applies it. The case study is our own earlier
analysis. We had reported that in 137 of 137 stale-state tracking failures, the correct state outranks
the stale state in at least one internal depth, and had drawn the encoded-but-unused conclusion. We
now report that this does not survive controls, and we retract it. We treat this as the natural test case
precisely because we know exactly how the number was produced and can re-derive every figure
from committed scripts.

Contributions.
1. We characterize the existence-over-depth statistic and show, in simulation with no model or data,
   that it saturates, that its label-swap null is degenerate, and that it is insensitive to effect size and
   sign (Section 3).
2. We specify the two distinct permutation nulls such claims require — a whole-example label swap,
   and an outcome shuffle with the maximum over depths taken inside each permutation, which is a
   max-T procedure [Westfall and Young, 1993, Nichols and Holmes, 2002] — and the depth bands
   that must be excluded to avoid circularity [Kriegeskorte et al., 2009].
3. We apply these to a real claim of our own and report that it fails all four controls, including a
   graded form significant in the direction opposite the claim (Sections 4–5).
4. We show how two of our own reported figures for the same quantity differ by four orders of
   magnitude purely through depth-set selection, and give the matched, selection-corrected re-analysis
   that isolates what actually survives.


2   Background and related work

Reading the residual stream. The logit lens [nostalgebraist, 2020] applies the model’s final norm
and unembedding to an intermediate residual state, effectively asking what the model would predict
if the remaining layers were the identity. The tuned lens [Belrose et al., 2023] fits an affine probe
per layer to correct the resulting bias. Yom Din et al. [2024] learn linear “shortcut” transformations
that map a hidden state to a later-layer representation, bypassing the intervening computation; this is
the closest published relative of the J-lens used in our case study, which linearizes the model’s own
remaining computation rather than fitting a probe. Geva et al. [2022] analyze feed-forward updates
directly in vocabulary space. All of these make intermediate states readable, and all of them invite
the existence-style inference we examine.

Probing and its controls. The methodological hazard is familiar from the probing literature. Hewitt
and Liang [2019] introduced control tasks to separate what a probe extracts from what it memorizes;
Ravichander et al. [2021] showed that a property can be decodable from representations without
being used by the model for its task; Belinkov [2022] surveys the shortcomings. Our point is the
analogue one level up: decodability at some depth, selected post hoc, needs a null and a control group
in the same way probe accuracy does.

Selection and circularity. Taking a maximum over depths and then reporting the winner is a
multiple-comparisons problem. The appropriate correction is a max-T permutation procedure, in
which the maximum is recomputed inside each permutation [Westfall and Young, 1993, Nichols
and Holmes, 2002]. Selecting a depth on the same data used to evaluate it is circular analysis
[Kriegeskorte et al., 2009], and the space of defensible-looking analysis choices — which lens, which
depths, which comparison group — is a garden of forking paths [Gelman and Loken, 2014].


