Table 1: The three runs. The 500-record run is a subset of the 5k dataset; the 137-failure run is its
complete failure population.



Split-half depth selection. Passing Null B shows that some depth separates the groups; it does not
show that a particular depth does. We select the best depth on one half of the data and evaluate it on
the held-out half, repeatedly, and report both the held-out effect and how often each depth is selected.
A measure whose selected depth is not reproducible across splits does not support a claim naming
that depth.

Excluded bands. Two ranges must be excluded before any “internal” claim. Concretely, for the 29
residual-stream positions of the case study we take the internal band to be depths 2–23, excluding
depths 0–1 as near-embedding and depths 24–28 as output-committed. Every later reference to
“the internal band” means exactly this range. Positions adjacent to the embedding carry little of the
model’s computation. Positions adjacent to the output are worse: at the final residual position the
lens is one norm and one unembedding away from the logits, so it is the model’s decision, and any
statistic separating outcomes there is circular [Kriegeskorte et al., 2009]. In the case study a group
separation measured at the last position is exactly 1.000 by construction.

4   Case study: stale-state tracking
Task and data. Each record narrates a sequence of entity movements and then asks where one
entity currently is. Exactly one relocation makes an earlier stated location stale; a failure is the model
emitting the stale room. The dataset is 5,000 synthetic records varying complexity level, entity type
(agent or object), and the number and position of distractor mentions.

Model and configuration. Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct in bfloat16, eager attention (required to capture
attention probabilities), no quantization, chat prompt format, with an entity-aware prefill ending
exactly where the room name must appear; the readout is the single token at that position. All three
runs used transformers 5.14.1. Per-run configurations are committed, and record 5,112 s of GPU
wall-clock in total (1.42 h): 3.52 and 3.49 s per record for the two 29-depth sweeps and 0.577 s per
record for the three-depth sweep. The GPU model is not recorded by the probe, so we do not state
one.

Depth indexing. The model has 28 decoder layers. A pre-hook on the input of each layer plus a
post-hook on the output of the last gives 29 residual-stream positions, depths 0..28; depth 28 is the
final residual stream, immediately before the final norm and unembedding. Attention is indexed by
decoder layer, 0..27, so no attention exists at depth 28 — a structural fact, not a capture failure. We
say “depths” rather than “layers” throughout. Ranks are 1-based and the vocabulary is V = 152,064.

Runs. Three runs, all from the same 5,000-record dataset (Table 1). Behaviourally the model
answers correctly on 97.1% of records, emits the stale state on 2.7% (137 records), and produces an
unparseable answer on 0.2%. Failures are entirely concentrated: the stale-output rate is 0.00% at
complexity levels 1 and 2 and 8.22% at level 3, and 0.00% for object entities against 5.47% for agents.
This concentration is what makes the matched comparison in Section 5 necessary rather than optional.
The 137-failure run is the complete failure population, re-swept at all depths. Reconstructing this
required signature matching, because that export renumbered example identifiers (Appendix A); the
twelve failures common to both full-depth runs agree exactly on every rank column, so the two pool.

Measures. Logit lens: unembed(norm(hℓ )). J-lens: a first-order expansion of the model’s own
remaining computation, fℓ (0) + Jhℓ , where fℓ runs decoder layers ℓ..28 with the real frozen KV
cache. Nine attention columns record mass at the readout position. For each measure we use the
margin between c and s.


