5.4                                                What survives

Table 3 gives the matched, selection-corrected picture. Two measures survive all three controls.
Attention mass differential at depth 22 separates the groups with matched AUC 0.973 and held-out
0.970, selecting depth 22 in 90% of splits; failures place more mass on the stale anchor and less on
the update mention. J-lens margin at depth 5 gives matched 0.888, held-out 0.882, depth 5 selected
in 96% of splits, replicating at 0.883 against the larger matched control set in the three-depth run.
Logit-lens margin does not survive. It clears Null B, but the depth it selects is reproducible in only
32% of splits, spreading across depths 12, 16, 18 and 23. The effect is real but diffuse, and no claim
naming a specific logit-lens depth is supported. This is precisely the distinction the split-half control
exists to draw, and it is invisible to a permutation test alone.

Independent per-head corroboration. The depth-22 result above is computed from per-example
attention aggregates. A separate per-head analysis, built from the raw attention tensors, ranks all 784
(depth, head) pairs by how much more a head prefers the stale anchor over the update mention in
failures than in successes. Of its top 20 heads, 15 fall in depths 19–22 and all 20 in depths 19–26,
with 9 at depth 22 alone; the highest-scoring single head is at depth 23. Because this statistic is
per-head and does not pass through the aggregates used above, it is a check on the depth-22 result
rather than a restatement of it. It also supplies the patching target in Appendix C.
The full attention sweep (Figure 4) locates discrimination at depths 19–25 and near chance through
the early and middle stack. Within the matched stratum two column pairs are identical, because
matched records carry a single stale mention, so the sweep covers seven distinct measures rather than
nine.


